// Blocks a correct peer never sends, on one ACK: one past SND.NXT, one reversed, one that starts at SND.UNA, and one
// that covers half a segment. None is an error, and none marks anything: the first three fail Linux's
// tcp_is_sackblock_valid and touch no entry; the last is valid, but a block marks only the entries it covers whole, as
// tcp_match_skb_to_sack leaves a buffer of one segment. So RACK's state does not move and nothing waits: had any block
// marked a segment, RACK's window of 10 / 4 = 2.5 ms would have sent the segments before it again at 23 ms, before the
// ACK at 30 ms the last line injects. The first block is no duplicate report: it starts above the ACK and the second
// does not contain it. Without timestamps four blocks fit, a segment carries 1460 bytes, the round trip comes from the
// handshake's ACK alone, and the budget is 512 * 1460 / 2 = 373760, stated as 373760 / 8 = 46720. Linux ignores the
// same blocks.
--tcp_timestamps=0
0 socket(..., SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP) = 3
+0 bind(3, ..., ...) = 0
+0 listen(3, 1) = 0

+0 < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 7>
+0 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 3>
+0.010 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 512
+0 accept(3, ..., ...) = 4

+0 write(4, ..., 100) = 100
+0 > P. 1:101(100) ack 1 win 46720
+0 write(4, ..., 100) = 100
+0 > P. 101:201(100) ack 1 win 46720
+0 write(4, ..., 100) = 100
+0 > P. 201:301(100) ack 1 win 46720
+0 write(4, ..., 100) = 100
+0 > P. 301:401(100) ack 1 win 46720
+0.010 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 512 <nop,nop,sack 301:501 251:201 1:101 201:251>
+0.010 < . 1:1(0) ack 401 win 512
